{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01C9UtvLRdwsLDZQFs37KADa","type":"tool_result","content":"685 specs/flashnext/evidence/dotfiles-observed.md\n24:d16cbd2f fleet: thunderbolt carries tensors only — admin takes the wire, and the library mount waits for reality (#240)\n25:b37dea0b fleet: the rail was up and 9x slow — every packet paid a C3 exit, a held fd is the cure (#238)\n67:| `enable` | 79 | `mkEnableOption` | **false** | \"PM QoS + MTU tuning for the coordinator↔worker fleet rails\" |\n68:| `peer` | 81–84 | `str` | *(no default — required)* | \"The peer's fast-rail /30 address, watched by the latency tripwire.\" |\n71:| `jumboInterfaces` | 114–141 | `listOf (submodule { name : str; mtu : int; })` | `[{thunderbolt0,65520} {thunderbolt1,65520} {enp191s0,9000}]` | lines 127–139 |\n73:There is no `enableTripwire`, no `sensorInterval`, no per-rail toggle. Five options total.\n106:- Script `mtuScript`, `modules/lowlat-cluster.nix:66-75`: `PATH=…iproute2`, then per interface `if [ -e /sys/class/net/<i> ]; then ip link set dev <i> mtu <n> 2>/dev/null || true; fi`. Idempotent, silent about down rails.\n115:coordinator $ for i in thunderbolt0 thunderbolt1 enp191s0; do cat /sys/class/net/$i/mtu; done\n117:worker      $ thunderbolt0 mtu=1500   enp191s0 mtu=1500\n127:| `description` | \"the fast rail's average RTT stays inside the PM QoS budget\" | 184 |\n138:Sensor, `:201-208`: `ping -q -i 0.01 -c 50 -W 2 <peer>`, tail -1, `awk -F'[/ ]' '{ printf \"%d rtt 1\\n\", $8 * 1000 }'` (avg field × 1000 → µs). A dark rail deliberately reports **0**, not a huge number — `:198-200`: \"darkness is tb-fleet-reachability's job and double-firing on one cable would only bury the signal that matters here.\"\n147:  tripwire-fleet-latency.service   loaded inactive dead   the fast rail's average RTT stays inside the PM QoS budget\n161:    peer = if config.networking.hostName == \"coordinator\" then \"10.99.0.2\" else \"10.99.0.1\";\n180:#   - 10.99.0.0/30  tb-fleet   (thunderbolt0) — the tensor rail, unchanged.\n181:#   - 10.99.1.0/30  eth-fleet  (enp191s0)     — this file, always-on.\n182:#   - 10.99.9.x/32  fleet IPs  (lo)           — the STABLE identity.\n184:So the third layer is `10.99.9.x/32` on **loopback**, and the ethernet rail is `10.99.1.x/30` — the campaign's guessed shape is correct.\n187:- coordinator: `10.99.0.1/30` tb (`modules/lowlat-cluster.nix:40`), `10.99.1.1/30` eth (`hosts/coordinator/eth-fleet.nix:67`), `10.99.9.1/32` lo (`hosts/coordinator/eth-fleet.nix:51`)\n188:- worker: `10.99.0.2/30` tb, `10.99.1.2/30` eth (`hosts/worker/default.nix:312`), `10.99.9.2/32` lo (`hosts/worker/default.nix:299`)\n190:Fleet identity is a dedicated oneshot, not `networking.localCommands` — `hosts/coordinator/eth-fleet.nix:42-53` and `hosts/worker/default.nix:292-301`, both `Type=oneshot`, `RemainAfterExit=true`, `ExecStart = ip addr replace 10.99.9.x/32 dev lo`, `after = network-pre.target`. Rationale at `eth-fleet.nix:42-43`: \"networking.localCommands is masked under NetworkManager (found the hard way on deploy night).\"\n194:- **eth-fleet route: metric 20**, declared. `hosts/coordinator/eth-fleet.nix:75` → `route1 = \"10.99.9.2/32,10.99.1.2,20\";` and `hosts/worker/default.nix:317` → `route1 = \"10.99.9.1/32,10.99.1.1,20\";`\n196:- **Thunderbolt route to the fleet IP: metric 50, IMPERATIVE, not in the tree.** `hosts/coordinator/eth-fleet.nix:17-18`: \"via TB at metric 50 (on the tb-fleet profile, added imperatively like the profile itself)\". `:28-29`: \"The imperative TB route stays at 50, untouched — only this profile's declared metric moved below it.\"\n201:coordinator $ ip route show | grep 10.99\n202:10.99.0.0/30 dev thunderbolt0 proto kernel scope link src 10.99.0.1 metric 101\n203:10.99.1.0/30 dev enp191s0     proto kernel scope link src 10.99.1.1 metric 103\n204:10.99.9.2 via 10.99.1.2 dev enp191s0     proto static metric 20\n205:10.99.9.2 via 10.99.0.2 dev thunderbolt0 proto static metric 50\n207:worker $ ip route show | grep 10.99\n208:10.99.0.0/30 dev thunderbolt0 proto kernel scope link src 10.99.0.2 metric 100\n209:10.99.1.0/30 dev enp191s0     proto kernel scope link src 10.99.1.2 metric 102\n210:10.99.9.1 via 10.99.1.1 dev enp191s0     proto static metric 20\n211:10.99.9.1 via 10.99.0.1 dev thunderbolt0 proto static metric 50\n213:`[M]` **Incidental**: the worker also carries a stale imperative `10.77.0.0/30 via 10.99.0.1 dev thunderbolt0 proto static metric 100` — the retired legacy /30 to the NAS. It exists in no committed file (`grep -rn \"10.77\" --include=*.nix .` hits only `modules/mesh-registry.nix:70` as an alias). Purely imperative residue.\n220:- **`workerRail` at `home/ssh.nix:97`:**\n222:  workerRail = if hostName == \"coordinator\" then \"10.99.9.2\" else \"worker\";\n224:- `mkBlock` at `:99-112`: `HostName = if target == \"worker\" then workerRail else target;` plus `User = \"tom\"`, `IdentityFile = \"~/.ssh/id_ed25519\"`, `IdentitiesOnly = true`, `StrictHostKeyChecking = \"yes\"`, and `ProxyJump = \"coordinator\"` only for `nas` from a non-coordinator/non-nas host (`needsJump`, `:60`).\n230:#   TB   10.99.0.2 : rtt min/avg/max = 33/58/122 us, mdev 18 us\n231:#   eth  10.99.1.2 : rtt min/avg/max = 58/72/142 us, mdev  9 us\n233:Ruling text at `:62-66`: \"Thunderbolt (10.99.0.x) is reserved for LLM-parallelism / tensor traffic ONLY; admin traffic … prefers the dedicated 5GbE cable (eth-fleet) and rides the stable fleet identity.\"\n241:aliases = [ \"worker\" \"10.42.0.5\" \"10.99.0.2\" \"10.99.1.2\" \"10.99.9.2\" ];\n245:`coordinator` at `:18-27`: aliases `[ \"coordinator\" \"10.99.1.1\" \"10.99.9.1\" ]` — **note: the coordinator row does NOT list `10.99.0.1` or `10.42.0.2`.** All five worker addresses carry the same pinned host key, so no TOFU on any rail. `modules/mesh.nix:25-28` turns the registry into `programs.ssh.knownHosts`; `:30-36` authorizes the user keys for `tom` and `root`.\n247:### 2.5 The deploy target — **still Thunderbolt** ⚠\n253:hostname = if host == \"worker\" then \"10.99.0.2\" else host;\n364:Network boundary (`modules/llama-swap.nix:82-91`): two interface-scoped firewall doors only — `tailscale0` and `wlp192s0`. **Neither `thunderbolt0` nor `enp191s0` admits :9292.**\n434:`hosts/coordinator/*` also carries: `hardware.nix` (820 B), `disko.nix`, and per-service files; `hosts/worker/*`: `hardware.nix` (1054 B — its `:18` comment notes thunderbolt0 is the fallback rail), `disko.nix`, `headless-display.nix`, `immich-ml.nix`, `journal-upload.nix`, `default.nix` (388 lines).\n469:  - `thunderbolt-ibverbs` → `hellas-ai/thunderbolt-ibverbs` rev `76ba39b630a70accb72f19388eefe48844b50eb8` — **the exact rev the recon doc names** (`docs/local-ai/ds4-vllm-recon-2026-08-21.md:26`)\n470:  - `linux-src` → `git.kernel.org/…/westeri/thunderbolt.git`, ref `refs/heads/next`, rev `503c5ae1e72aa9ed91925dafa3d82ee2e992747f` — **the exact patched-core rev the recon doc names** (`:26`)\n472:- **`nixosModules`** (upstream `flake.nix:513-532`): `default` (imports `thunderbolt-ibverbs.nixosModules.default` + applies its overlay), `thunderbolt-ibverbs`, `rpc-server`, `benchmark-executor`, `benchmark-runner`, `ec-su-axb35`, `fastflowlm`, `smu-exporter`, `npu-exporter`, `ryzenadj`, `tuning`.\n474:- **Packages exposing `linux-thunderbolt`, `linux-thunderbolt-dev`, `linux-thunderbolt-modules`, `thunderbolt-ibverbs`, `thunderbolt-ibverbs-linux-thunderbolt`, `thunderbolt-ibverbs-bench-tools`, `thunderbolt-ibverbs-perftest`** (upstream `flake.nix:305-318`, `:657-658`, `:701-706`).\n475:- **A ready-made TP=2 pair harness**: `strix-halo-vllm-pair-bench-<s>` (upstream `flake.nix:712-714`, package at `pkgs/strix-halo-vllm-pair-bench/default.nix`, 9484 B), built on `vllmPairBenchEnv = symlinkJoin [ pkgs.vllm-rocm … ]` \"so the vLLM closure exposes both `vllm` and `ray`\" (`:683-689`). Its scenarios include `qwen-peak | llama-tp2-win | qwen35-122b-awq-capacity | qwen35-122b-awq-prime | minimax-m27-awq-strix-2h`; it takes `MASTER`/`WORKER`, `TRANSPORTS` including `usb4_rdma` and `lan_tcp`, and exports `TB_IFNAME=thunderbolt0`, `USB4_HCA`, and a full NCCL env block (`NCCL_IB_GID_INDEX=1`, `NCCL_MIN_NCHANNELS=4`, …) — `pkgs/strix-halo-vllm-pair-bench/default.nix:140-235` **[S]**.\n492:- **Firewall.** `networking.firewall.trustedInterfaces = [ \"enp191s0\" ]` on both boxes (`hosts/coordinator/eth-fleet.nix:80`, `hosts/worker/default.nix:321`) — **`thunderbolt0` is NOT trusted on either host**, and `flake.nix:725-726` asserts `wlp192s0` is never trusted. Ray/NCCL/ar2 ports (recon doc names 18515/18531, `:70`) on 10.99.0.x would need explicit admissions.\n494:- **The MTU decision.** `modules/lowlat-cluster.nix:49-54` explicitly parks the second TB rail and jumbo frames: \"a TP=2 decode all-reduce is a ~5 KB payload, i.e. latency-bound, not bandwidth-bound. When that changes, it belongs in tb-fleet.nix beside the rail it extends.\"\n509:| `boot.kernelModules = [\"thunderbolt-net\"]` | `:110` | `:220` |\n510:| peer address | `peer = \"10.99.0.2\"` binding at `:49` | hardcoded `10.99.0.1` at `:236`, `:241` |\n519:2. XDomain peer present (`ls /sys/bus/thunderbolt/devices/ | grep -qE '^[0-9]+-[1-9]'`) → `nmcli connection up tb-fleet || true`.\n520:3. Retimers present but no peer (`grep -qE '^[0-9]+-[0-9]+:'`) → unbind + `sleep 2` + rebind both NHIs on `/sys/bus/pci/drivers/thunderbolt`.\n538:Sensors: TB pings `10.99.0.2` (`tb-fleet.nix:143`), eth pings `10.99.1.2` (`eth-fleet.nix:98`) — both report `1` when dark. The TB tripwire deliberately keeps pinging the /30, not the fleet IP (`eth-fleet.nix:36-39`: \"it watches the FAST rail specifically\").\n553:### 7.1 ⚠ \"deploy-rs dials the worker at 10.99.0.2 over Thunderbolt at flake.nix:536 guarded by asserts at 1194/1227\" — **THIS IS STILL THE CURRENT STATE. It was NOT replaced by #240.**\n559:hostname = if host == \"worker\" then \"10.99.0.2\" else host;\n561:with the comment block `:525-535` still reading \"except the worker, which is dialled by it