at 64 / 4096 / 16384 / 65536 B, the allreduce-shaped simultaneous exchange at 8192 / 16384 / 65536 B, throughput both directions, `ring_size` and `throttling` as read from configfs, resolved device paths on both ends, and `open_count` (assert exactly 1 per side). `loop: \"python\"` is stated for honesty about syscall-loop overhead. |\ndocs/MORNING.md:102:| `skipped:REASON` | One of `skipped:serve-up-on-shared-cable`, `skipped:rail-peer-unreachable`, `skipped:configfs-group-missing`. Checked before any device access. |\ndocs/MORNING.md:207:6. **The stream primitive's worth as a transport** — decided against the rule\ndocs/MORNING.md:296:- **Step:** <checkpoint or lane id, e.g. cp-tp2 / cp-usb4stream>\ndocs/MORNING.md:403:| **The ncclNet-plugin route for the stream primitive** | Rejected **for the latency goal**: it sits under the collective library's proxy/protocol stack, which eats most of the win; HopID scarcity forces a multiplexer over one or two long-lived streams; and comm teardown on every serve restart is exactly the open/close pattern the wedge hazard forbids. 3–5 attended days to a fragile prototype. The doorbell-allreduce port (item 6) is the real path. |\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:45:hard read-only rule toward live device state (no `/dev/tbstream*` opens, no\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:46:configfs writes, no reboots, no serves — the wedge hazard in §5 is why):\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:52:| usb4stream-depth | the operator-demanded *second, independent* deliberation, instructed to attack the first one rather than defer to it |\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:368:/dev/tbstream2**; cable B's parked service carries `fn0` index=0, `fn1` index=1.\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:369:On the worker, cable A is its domain1 and its `fn0` is **/dev/tbstream0**. So:\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:371:1. **The same logical stream is `/dev/tbstream2` on one box and `/dev/tbstream0`\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:373:2. **The coordinator's `/dev/tbstream0` and `1` are PEERLESS** — they belong to\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:374:   cable B, whose worker counterpart has no configfs groups at all. A blocking\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:381:`/dev/tbstream$index`, with **both ends' `fn0` groups required to exist before\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:383:`/dev/tbstream[0-9]` literal anywhere.\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:397:  stream primitive offers ~10 usable HopIDs, **shared with thunderbolt_net's own\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:430:delivers nothing the communicator hook doesn't); and using tbstream for the\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:449:**5.6 What shipped:** one authoring lane (`usb4stream-bench`) plus a dead-last\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:450:checkpoint (`cp-usb4stream`, after cp-close) with three hard skip preconditions\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:473:stream primitive loses on bandwidth (≤841 MB/s per stream, less at ring 1024) to\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:794:| stream primitive RTT, 64 B / 4 KB p50 | 14.3 µs / 21.8 µs | measured, twins, Python syscall loop |\nhost/rdma/attended-bringup.md:365:sudo modprobe configfs ib_core ib_uverbs      # -a form if your modprobe needs it for multiple names\nsilent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json:306:      \"goal\": \"AMENDED 2026-08-30: docs/MORNING.md currently holds the operator's 2026-08-29 day-run stop-state record — FIRST move that file verbatim to docs/DAYRUN-STOP-STATE-2026-08-29.md (git mv, keep every byte), THEN write the new docs/MORNING.md per spec claim 7.1 (specs/flashnext/spec.md#r7). This lane renders BEFORE the milestone checkpoints run (it sits early in the serial graph), so the receipt review table has two row kinds: receipts already banked at render time (build, weights, possibly smoke) carry their observed status and the bound they were graded against; every receipt that lands AFTER render is PRE-DECLARED — one row each for preflight, proxy, tp2, residency, fidelity, context, bench, and usb4stream, naming the file path, the bound scripts/receipts-verify.py grades it against, and how to read it. Three pre-declared rows are mandatory and specific: (a) results/receipts/usb4stream.json — cp-usb4stream runs dead-last after cp-close and only when the pair serve is already down; name its three typed outcomes (ok / skipped:REASON / aborted:PHASE:ERRNO), say that data.outcome is where to read them, that 'skipped:serve-up-on-shared-cable' is the EXPECTED outcome after a healthy night, and that a skipped or aborted outcome is the typed blocker for that step per F.12; (b) results/receipts/failed/ — the quarantine directory: any step that fails its own grading writes its fail receipt THERE so one graded failure cannot redden every later gate run; every file present there in the morning is a typed blocker the operator reviews first; (c) results/receipts/failed/context.json — if present, the honest full-context decode ratio undershot the 0.9 bound (12 full-attention layers walking 256K KV; the community precedent shows about a 2x falloff by 50-73k) — a typed performance finding routed to the optimization menu, deliberately NOT a campaign failure. Also render: the open-items list (every UNKNOWN from the spec with its drain state); the promotion checklist (what the operator verifies before any fleet change, per ruling P13 — and state the causality plainly: the fleet's local-models sync retires any staged artifact that has no catalog row; it pruned the staged checkpoint from BOTH nodes on 08-29 around 20:07/20:14; applying handoff/catalog-row.patch is the act that makes staging durable, and it must precede any rebuild or any restart of the model-sync trigger service); the transport-rung caveat (if any receipt records fn_transport_rung wire-fallback, the night ran on the 5GbE wire: valid but degraded receipts, and bench.json then does NOT satisfy the rail-sockets Gate 0 for verbs bring-up — healing rail 0 is the morning's first transport act); the optimization menu; and a blocker template implementing ruling P16. The optimization menu carries, beyond the original items (tuned mixture-kernel config generation for this GPU, graph-capture matrix, jumbo-frame A-B, draft-length sweep, the four-bit expert lane pointers from IMPORTS.md): the speculative-head depth sweep n in 1 to 4 plus the index-share and padded-drafter knobs (UNKNOWN-5); the attended RDMA go/no-go with the honest cost (2-4 attended hours, two more reboots, worker deploy-path fix first, nothing staged for kernel 7.2.2 yet) and honest expectation (about +3.4 percent measured precedent over held TCP); the stream-primitive morning decision rule (attended port of the reference doorbell allreduce onto the in-tree stream device ONLY if the banked exchange p50 at 8-16 KiB is at or under about 40 microseconds AND the bench matrix shows decode is allreduce-dominated — noting the stream bench likely runs attended in the morning after teardown, since it skips while the pair serves); the full-context decode probe follow-up when deferred; a rejected-with-reason register so nothing is re-litigated (the community int4-WMMA expert lane: bound to a different runtime's artifact, single-device only, unreachable even in its own public release; the indexer rotation plus FP4 QAT art: fidelity art for a different model family's trained indexer graph — our engine's indexer is weight-free bf16 with no such trained graph, so transplanting it would corrupt top-k selection); the load-phase page-cache drop-behind remedy pointer for repo issue 2 (release file pages in chunks behind the loader); a per-shape kernel block-size tuning item for the 2560x640 and 640x2560 expert shapes (community precedent: a forced tile choice beat the auto-selector on exactly these shapes); and a note to audit the drafter hidden-state versus prefix-cache position sync before the speculative profile is declared production (a community v1.1 fix records the failure mode: cached target prefix reused while the drafter state timeline sits elsewhere breaks multi-turn — the operator files the repo issue). Also write docs/ANNOUNCE.md: a short community post draft describing the setup (two 128 GB unified-memory desktops, two fast rails, one control wire), what was built overnight, where the evidence lives, and where benchmarks land. Boundaries: touch only docs/MORNING.md, docs/ANNOUNCE.md, and docs/DAYRUN-STOP-STATE-2026-08-29.md.\",\nsilent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json:339:            \"test -s docs/DAYRUN-STOP-STATE-2026-08-29.md && grep -q 'Day-run stop-state' docs/DAYRUN-STOP-STATE-2026-08-29.md && grep -q 'operator-called halt' docs/DAYRUN-STOP-STATE-2026-08-29.md && ! grep -q 'Day-run stop-state' docs/MORNING.md && grep -q 'catalog-row.patch' docs/MORNING.md && grep -q 'usb4stream' docs/MORNING.md\"\nsilent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json:531:      \"id\": \"usb4stream-bench\",\nsilent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json:534:      \"goal\": \"Author (never run — the dead-last checkpoint runs it) a wedge-safe first-light benchmark of the in-tree USB4 stream primitive, as transport-decision evidence for the morning. Deliverables: (1) bench/usb4stream-bench.py — one file serving both roles (coordinator role is the default; peer role via --role peer --dev PATH; the peer is launched by streaming this same file over the control wire: ssh worker with the script on stdin, never a copy). Non-negotiable mechanics, each existing because an open/close storm against a mismatched peer wedges router hop tables and already darkened rail 0 once: (a) NEVER hardcode a numbered device node and never write a digit directly after the word tbstream anywhere in the file — the numbering is asymmetric across the twins and the coordinator carries PEERLESS cable-B groups at the low indices, where a blocking open waits forever; resolve on EACH node as: the rail netdev carrying the 10.99.0.0/30 address, then readlink /sys/class/net/RAIL/device, then the parent xdomain, then the sibling service whose key attribute is stream, then the configfs group fn0's index attribute, then the device node /dev/tbstream suffixed with that index; require BOTH ends' fn0 configfs groups to exist before any open. (b) Exactly ONE open attempt per side, ever, blocking, under a 30 s alarm; any failure after that point: close whatever is open, kill the ssh child, write the receipt, exit — never reopen, never retry, never write configfs (read ring_size and throttling only). (c) Idempotence guard: if results/receipts/usb4stream.json already exists, exit 0 immediately WITHOUT touching any device — this is what makes harness retries storm-free by construction. (d) Skip preconditions, checked in this order BEFORE any device access, each producing receipt status pass with data.outcome 'skipped:REASON' and exit 0: (i) the pair serve is up — the API answers on the coordinator, or podman reports the serving container running on either node (check the worker over the control wire) — outcome 'skipped:serve-up-on-shared-cable': the stream device shares cable A with the serving rails and a wedge would take the campaign's headline deliverable down, so this bench runs ONLY on an idle cable; (ii) ping -c1 -W2 to the rail peer fails — outcome 'skipped:rail-peer-unreachable'; (iii) either end's fn0 configfs group is missing — outcome 'skipped:configfs-group-missing'. Record data.serve_up true or false in EVERY receipt including skips. (e) Fixed schedule, no adaptivity, no retries: 50 iterations of 4 KiB ping-pong warmup (discarded); RTT at 64, 4096, 16384, 65536 bytes times 500 iterations with p50 and p99 per size; the allreduce-shaped simultaneous exchange at 8192, 16384, 65536 bytes times 500 (both sides write N bytes then read N bytes — ring 1024 times 4 KiB frames buffers this safely); throughput 256 MiB each direction; under 90 s wall total, whole run under a global timeout. (f) The receipt at results/receipts/usb4stream.json: one JSON object, step 'usb4stream', status ALWAYS 'pass' — this bench is evidence-gathering, not a campaign claim, so a mid-run abort is typed as data.outcome 'aborted:PHASE:ERRNO' rather than a fail status that would redden every later gate run; data carries outcome, per-size rtt and exchange p50/p99 fields, throughput both directions, ring_size and throttling as read from configfs, resolved device paths on both ends, open_count (assert exactly 1 per side), serve_up, and loop 'python' for honesty about syscall-loop overhead. (2) scripts/receipts-verify.py: add a 'usb4stream' entry to the BOUNDS table — outcome 'ok' requires open_count 1 and all schedule fields present; 'skipped:' and 'aborted:' outcomes require their reason text and pass. (3) docs/USB4STREAM-TRANSPORT.md — the decision memo: why the collective-library net-plugin route is REJECTED for the latency goal (it sits under the library's proxy and protocol stack, HopID scarcity shared with the network paths forces a multiplexer over one or two long-lived streams, and comm teardown on every serve restart is exactly the open/close pattern the wedge hazard forbids; 3-5 attended days to a fragile prototype, 2-4 attended weeks to overnight-trustworthy); why the real path is a 2-4 attended-day port of the reference doorbell-plus-progress-thread allreduce (the 105 microsecond reference bar) from verbs onto the stream device's read and write, with the receive path run by the progress thread; the morning trigger criteria (banked exchange p50 at 8-16 KiB at or under about 40 microseconds AND the bench matrix showing allreduce-dominated decode); the note that after a healthy night the first real run happens attended in the morning AFTER the pair is torn down (the serve-up skip is deliberate); and a drafted-not-filed issue body for the port. (4) tests/test_usb4stream_bench.py: py-compile both edited python files, assert the no-hardcoded-device-node invariant (no digit after tbstream in the bench source), assert the idempotence guard and the serve-up skip are present, and exercise the receipts checker's three outcome branches with synthetic receipt dicts. Boundaries: touch only bench/usb4stream-bench.py, docs/USB4STREAM-TRANSPORT.md, scripts/receipts-verify.py, and tests/.\",","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7dad2f56-3033-4a9b-8df9-7c3428d04797","uuid":"822331df-3853-4ead-9342-b0eae8b382c2","timestamp":"2026-08-30T03:51:52.300Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":".git:1:gitdir: /home/tom/mecattaf/flashnext/.git/worktrees/usb4stream-bench\nREADME.md:21:measured numbers for the in-tree USB4 stream primitive. Benchmarks land in\nREADME.md:64:| Transport | RCCL over TCP sockets on rail 0 (`thunderbolt0`, 10.99.0.x/30), **single rail, cable A**. `NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1` unconditional. Terminal fallback rung: the 5 GbE wire, loudly receipted. RDMA is the attended morning A/B behind Gate 0; the in-tree USB4 stream primitive is measured (not ridden) tonight — full ladder below | `host/fn-env.sh`, `host/rdma/` |\nREADME.md:89:- [ ] usb4stream-bench + cp-usb4stream (dead-last, wedge-safe, idle-cable-only)\nREADME.md:223:The in-tree stream primitive (`thunderbolt_stream`, by the Thunderbolt\nREADME.md:224:maintainer, kernel 7.2+, `/dev/tbstreamN` over raw NHI DMA rings — no IP\nREADME.md:241:  `docs/USB4STREAM-TRANSPORT.md` (authored by the `usb4stream-bench` lane).\nREADME.md:243:  single-open, fixed-schedule, retry-proof bench (`bench/usb4stream-bench.py`)\nREADME.md:244:  plus a dead-last checkpoint (`cp-usb4stream`, after cp-close) that runs\nREADME.md:336:cp-tp2, cp-bench (21600 s budget), cp-close, cp-usb4stream (dead-last).\nREADME.md:383:- **The in-tree USB4 stream primitive is real and fast** (14.3 µs 64 B RTT,\ndocs/MORNING.md:84:| `results/receipts/usb4stream.json` | `bench/usb4stream-bench.py` (cp-usb4stream) | added by the `usb4stream-bench` lane: `outcome == \"ok\"` requires `open_count == 1` and every schedule field present; `skipped:` / `aborted:` outcomes require their reason text and pass | **See the dedicated row below.** |\ndocs/MORNING.md:86:#### `results/receipts/usb4stream.json` — read `data.outcom